                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which Delhi IT office handles reassessment?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Jurisdictional AO at Civic Centre, Aayakar Bhawan, C.R. Building or NeAC for faceless cases. Search-related at ARA Centre. International taxation at Civic Centre. Specific office depends on ward/circle jurisdiction.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I challenge a Section 148 notice in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Grounds include: 148A non-compliance, absence of 'information', time limit violation under S.149, change of opinion, lack of approval. Objections to AO or writ petition before Delhi High Court. Patron evaluates strongest ground for each case.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the time limit for reopening?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>3 years from end of AY (normal). Up to 10 years if escaped income >= Rs 50 lakh with documentary evidence. Property transactions and large cash deposits are the most common triggers for extended-period reassessments in Delhi.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is Section 148A and how does it protect taxpayers?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Mandatory pre-notice inquiry (since Finance Act 2021). AO must: issue show cause, disclose information, consider response, pass speaking order. Notice issued without following this is invalid. Delhi HC has quashed numerous notices for 148A non-compliance.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What are penalties if escaped income is found?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Interest under S.234A/B/C on additional income. Concealment penalty 100%-300% under S.271(1)(c). Misreporting 200% under S.270A(8). Prosecution under S.276C for wilful evasion (up to 7 years). Patron structures defence to prevent penalty from the outset.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can previously scrutinised return be reopened?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes, but only with genuinely new information not available during original scrutiny. Reopening on same facts with different interpretation is 'change of opinion' - invalid. Delhi HC has consistently quashed such reopenings.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Should I file return or challenge the notice first?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Both simultaneously. File return to avoid best judgment assessment. File objections challenging validity. Filing return doesn't mean accepting reassessment. If notice is clearly invalid, Delhi HC writ can quash it.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
